WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.429
Welcome. In this class, we are going to replace a few misleading

2
00:00:06.429 --> 00:00:12.857
shortcuts with a practical mental model for language models. By the end,

3
00:00:12.857 --> 00:00:19.286
you should be able to separate training from inference, describe how text

4
00:00:19.286 --> 00:00:25.714
is generated step by step, identify the parts of a larger AI

5
00:00:25.714 --> 00:00:32.143
system that shape an answer, and explain why confident wording is not

6
00:00:32.143 --> 00:00:38.571
evidence. You do not need mathematics or programming experience. You only need

7
00:00:38.571 --> 00:00:45.000
to notice which part of the system is doing which job.

8
00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:52.308
Start with the difference between training and inference. During training, a model

9
00:00:52.308 --> 00:00:59.615
processes many examples and adjusts numerical parameters so that it becomes better

10
00:00:59.615 --> 00:01:06.923
at predicting patterns. Those parameters are not a tidy library of copied

11
00:01:06.923 --> 00:01:14.231
pages. They are learned behavior compressed into a very large set of

12
00:01:14.231 --> 00:01:21.538
numbers. That distinction matters because a trained model can generalize beyond its

13
00:01:21.538 --> 00:01:28.846
examples, but it can also fail, reproduce undesirable patterns, or produce a

14
00:01:28.846 --> 00:01:36.154
claim that was never present in a source. Inference is what happens

15
00:01:36.154 --> 00:01:43.462
when you use the trained model. Your current instructions and context are

16
00:01:43.462 --> 00:01:50.769
processed with the learned parameters to produce a response. A product may

17
00:01:50.769 --> 00:01:58.077
surround the model with search, retrieval, files, memory, or tools. Those features

18
00:01:58.077 --> 00:02:05.385
can provide current evidence or perform actions, but they are separate system

19
00:02:05.385 --> 00:02:12.692
capabilities. Their presence must be established and their results must still be

20
00:02:12.692 --> 00:02:20.000
checked.

21
00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:27.222
Here is a quick diagnostic. Imagine an assistant gives you yesterday's weather

22
00:02:27.222 --> 00:02:34.444
without using a weather tool or a supplied report. Do not say

23
00:02:34.444 --> 00:02:41.667
that inference has searched a live database unless the product actually provides

24
00:02:41.667 --> 00:02:48.889
that capability. The base model is applying learned behavior to the current

25
00:02:48.889 --> 00:02:56.111
context. Now imagine the same assistant calls a weather service and returns

26
00:02:56.111 --> 00:03:03.333
a timestamped result. The trained model may decide how to ask for

27
00:03:03.333 --> 00:03:10.556
the data and how to explain it, while the tool supplies the

28
00:03:10.556 --> 00:03:17.778
current observation. The answer is a system result, not evidence that the

29
00:03:17.778 --> 00:03:25.000
model's training contained yesterday's weather.

30
00:03:25.000 --> 00:03:32.308
Next, look at generation. Text is represented as tokens, which may be

31
00:03:32.308 --> 00:03:39.615
whole words, pieces of words, punctuation, or other symbols. Given the conversation

32
00:03:39.615 --> 00:03:46.923
so far, a language model estimates possible next tokens. Its decoding process

33
00:03:46.923 --> 00:03:54.231
selects one, that token becomes part of the context, and the model

34
00:03:54.231 --> 00:04:01.538
repeats the process. This is more useful than saying the system looks

35
00:04:01.538 --> 00:04:08.846
up a stored sentence. Learned patterns can support coherent explanations, plans, code,

36
00:04:08.846 --> 00:04:16.154
and stories that were not copied as complete answers. The same process

37
00:04:16.154 --> 00:04:23.462
can also continue a plausible but unsupported direction. Suppose the unfinished sentence

38
00:04:23.462 --> 00:04:30.769
is, The capital of France is. A likely continuation is Paris. That

39
00:04:30.769 --> 00:04:38.077
does not mean every likely continuation in every domain is true. Generation

40
00:04:38.077 --> 00:04:45.385
rewards a continuation that fits learned patterns and current context. Verification is

41
00:04:45.385 --> 00:04:52.692
the separate job of establishing whether the resulting claim is correct for

42
00:04:52.692 --> 00:05:00.000
your purpose.

43
00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:06.667
Checkpoint: explain in one sentence why a plausible continuation can still be

44
00:05:06.667 --> 00:05:13.333
factually wrong. Use the words generation and verification in your answer. Pause

45
00:05:13.333 --> 00:05:20.000
the class while you answer.

46
00:06:50.000 --> 00:06:55.833
A strong answer separates two jobs: generation produces a continuation that fits

47
00:06:55.833 --> 00:07:01.667
learned patterns and the supplied context; verification tests whether the claim is

48
00:07:01.667 --> 00:07:07.500
supported and correct. If your answer treated fluent wording as proof, revise

49
00:07:07.500 --> 00:07:13.333
it now. Fluency can make a statement easy to read, but it

50
00:07:13.333 --> 00:07:19.167
does not provide a source, a test result, or a calibrated confidence

51
00:07:19.167 --> 00:07:25.000
measure.

52
00:07:25.000 --> 00:07:33.500
The model is only one part of the result you see. Instructions

53
00:07:33.500 --> 00:07:42.000
define the task and boundaries. Context supplies facts, examples, files, prior messages,

54
00:07:42.000 --> 00:07:50.500
and tool results. The product can add policies, retrieval, tool access, output

55
00:07:50.500 --> 00:07:59.000
validation, and a particular model or decoding configuration. When an answer is

56
00:07:59.000 --> 00:08:07.500
weak, diagnose the system before rewriting the same prompt repeatedly. Missing facts

57
00:08:07.500 --> 00:08:16.000
may require authoritative context or retrieval. A calculation may require a suitable

58
00:08:16.000 --> 00:08:24.500
tool and a checked result. Conflicting instructions may require a clear authority

59
00:08:24.500 --> 00:08:33.000
order. A high-impact decision may require a human owner even when the

60
00:08:33.000 --> 00:08:41.500
draft is excellent. Model choice matters, but changing the model is not

61
00:08:41.500 --> 00:08:50.000
a substitute for fixing a missing evidence source or an unsafe workflow.

62
00:08:50.000 --> 00:08:56.250
Try a diagnosis. An assistant is asked to summarize a policy change,

63
00:08:56.250 --> 00:09:02.500
but the new policy was never supplied and the product has no

64
00:09:02.500 --> 00:09:08.750
retrieval tool. Choose the missing element: clearer tone, more evidence, faster generation,

65
00:09:08.750 --> 00:09:15.000
or stronger confidence. Then name the safest next step.

66
00:11:15.000 --> 00:11:23.000
Finally, separate capability, style, and correctness. A capable model can produce clear

67
00:11:23.000 --> 00:11:31.000
natural language across many tasks. That capability does not turn confident tone

68
00:11:31.000 --> 00:11:39.000
into a probability. The sentence I am certain may itself be generated

69
00:11:39.000 --> 00:11:47.000
because that style fits the context. For factual work, ask which claims

70
00:11:47.000 --> 00:11:55.000
matter, identify authoritative sources, compare the claims with those sources, run relevant

71
00:11:55.000 --> 00:12:03.000
tests, and record unresolved uncertainty. For creative work, evaluate usefulness, originality, audience

72
00:12:03.000 --> 00:12:11.000
fit, and constraints instead of pretending there is one factual answer. A

73
00:12:11.000 --> 00:12:19.000
safe workflow matches the verification method to the consequence of being wrong.

74
00:12:19.000 --> 00:12:27.000
A casual brainstorming idea may need lightweight review. Medical, legal, financial, security,

75
00:12:27.000 --> 00:12:35.000
or operational decisions require qualified evidence and accountable human judgment.

76
00:12:35.000 --> 00:12:41.667
You now have the four-part mental model: training creates learned behavior; inference

77
00:12:41.667 --> 00:12:48.333
uses it; generation proceeds step by step; and the surrounding system plus

78
00:12:48.333 --> 00:12:55.000
verification determines whether an output is useful and trustworthy. Next, compare two

79
00:12:55.000 --> 00:13:01.667
outputs from the same low-risk task. Label each factual claim as supported,

80
00:13:01.667 --> 00:13:08.333
unsupported, or needing verification. Also record which weaknesses can be fixed with

81
00:13:08.333 --> 00:13:15.000
better context and which require a tool, source, test, or human expert.

82
00:16:25.000 --> 00:16:31.250
When you are ready, begin the knowledge check. You will distinguish training

83
00:16:31.250 --> 00:16:37.500
from inference, identify token-by-token generation, recognize retrieval and tools as surrounding capabilities,

84
00:16:37.500 --> 00:16:43.750
and choose evidence over confident tone. You can review the transcript or

85
00:16:43.750 --> 00:16:50.000
return to any section before submitting.

86
00:16:50.000 --> 00:16:57.500
Remember the shortest version: a language model generates; a trustworthy workflow verifies.

87
00:16:57.500 --> 00:17:05.000
Keep those jobs separate, and you will diagnose AI results more accurately.
